    "content": "試分辨「藝術元素」與「藝術原則」，並各舉三項。\n再以「一幅畫中人物置於畫面正中央」為例，說明此安排同時涉及哪一項元素與哪一項原則。",
    "explanation": "元素與原則的分辨是本科的基礎，而混淆兩者是評賞題最常見的失分來源 —— 把「平衡」說成元素，或把「色彩」說成原則，即顯示未掌握這一組概念的結構。記住那句「元素是用了什麼、原則是怎樣安排」，即可即時判別。\n最後一段的補充是本題的加分位：置中的效果並非固定，而取決於其餘元素的配合。評賞題若把某項安排的效果說成必然如此，通常會被視為分析不足；良好的答案會指出效果的條件。",
    "referenceAnswer": "分辨：\n藝術元素是構成作品的基本材料，是作者實際落在畫面上的東西 —— 線條、形狀、色彩、明暗、質感、空間、量感。\n藝術原則是組織這些元素的方式，是元素之間的關係 —— 平衡、對比、比例、節奏、統一、強調、變化。\n可以一句話概括：元素是「用了什麼」，原則是「怎樣安排」。元素可以逐一指認，原則則須觀察多個元素之間的關係方能判斷。\n\n三項元素：線條、色彩、明暗。\n三項原則：平衡、對比、強調。\n\n人物置於畫面正中央：\n所涉元素為【空間】—— 人物在畫面之中所佔的位置與周圍留白的分佈，屬空間的運用。\n所涉原則為【平衡】與【強調】—— 置中令左右兩側的視覺重量相等，屬對稱平衡；同時，畫面中心是視線最自然落點，置中即把該人物置於最受注視的位置，屬強調。\n\n須留意，同一個安排可以同時服務多項原則，而其效果亦視乎其餘元素而定：若人物置中而背景空曠，強調的效果最為顯著；若周圍佈滿細節，置中的位置優勢便會被削弱。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "The elements of art are the basic materials of a work, what the artist actually puts on the surface — line, shape, colour, value, texture, space, form. The principles of design are the ways those elements are organised, the relations between them — balance, contrast, proportion, rhythm, unity, emphasis, variety.\nIn a sentence: elements are what was used, principles are how it was arranged. Elements can be pointed to individually; principles can only be judged by observing relations among several elements.\nThree elements: line, colour, value. Three principles: balance, contrast, emphasis.\nA figure at the centre: the element involved is space — the position the figure occupies and the distribution of the surrounding void. The principles are balance and emphasis: centring equalises the visual weight on either side, giving symmetrical balance, while the centre is where the eye naturally falls, so placing the figure there gives it the most attention.\nNote that one arrangement can serve several principles at once, and its effect depends on the other elements: centring against an empty background emphasises strongly, while surrounding detail weakens that positional advantage.",
    "contentEn": "Distinguish the elements of art from the principles of design, giving three of each.\nThen, taking a painting in which the figure is placed at the centre, say which element and which principle are involved.",
    "explanationEn": "Distinguishing elements from principles is foundational, and confusing them is the commonest loss in appreciation questions — calling balance an element, or colour a principle, shows the structure of the pair has not been grasped. \"Elements are what was used, principles are how it was arranged\" settles it immediately.\nThe closing note earns the extra credit: the effect of centring is not fixed but depends on the other elements. An appreciation answer that states an effect as inevitable usually reads as under-analysed; a strong one names the conditions under which the effect holds."
